<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.14.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01495</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.14.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4a6ba3b1-65b5-41cb-af34-08e3cde60da6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this
      lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Functions \(a, b, c, d, e\), and \(f\) are given below. Which functions are linear? <emphasis>Select the three</emphasis> answers that apply. </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>
    \(a(x)=3x\)
  </item>
  <item>
    \(b(x)=3^x\)
  </item>
  <item>
    \(c(x)=x^3\)
  </item>
  <item>
    \(d(x)=9+3x\)
  </item>
  <item>
    \(e(x)=9 \cdot 3^x\)
  </item>
  <item>\(
    f(x)=9 \cdot 3x\)


  </item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>

<list>
  <item>\(a(x)=3x\)</item>
  <item>\(d(x)=9+3x\)</item>
  <item>\(f(x)=9⋅3x\)</item>
</list>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Functions \(a, b, c, d, e\), and \(f\) are given below. Which functions are exponential? <emphasis>Select the two</emphasis> answers
    that apply. </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(a(x)=3x\)</item>
  <item> \(b(x)=3^x\) </item>
  <item> \(c(x)=x^3\) </item>
  <item> \(d(x)=9+3x\) </item>
  <item> \(e(x)=9 \cdot 3^x\) </item>
  <item>\(
    f(x)=9 \cdot 3x\) </item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<list>
  <item>\(b(x)=3^x\)</item>
  <item>\(e(x)=9 \cdot 3^x\)</item>
</list>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here are 4 equations defining 4 different functions, \(a, b, c\), and \(d\). List them in order of increasing rate
    of change. That is, start with the one that grows the slowest and end with the one that grows the quickest.

    <list>
      <item>\(a(x)=5x+3\)</item>
      <item>\(b(x)=3x+5\)</item>
      <item>\(c(x)=x+4\)</item>
      <item>\(d(x)=1+4x\)</item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>Option 1: \(a(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(c(x)\), \(d(x)\)</item>
  <item>Option 2: \(d(x)\), \(c(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(a(x)\)</item>
  <item>Option 3: \(a(x)\),\(d(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(a(x)\)</item>
  <item>Option 4: \(c(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(d(x)\), \(a(x)\)
  </item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Option 4: \(c(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(d(x)\), \(a(x)\)
</para>
<para>Use the given information for problems 4 – 6. Technology is required. Function \(f\) is defined by \(f(x)=3x+5\),
  and function \(g\) is defined by \(g(x)=(1.1)^x\).</para>
  <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        </entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
          \(f(x)\)
        </entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
          \(g(x)\)
        </entry>
      </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">
          1
        </entry>
        <entry/>
        <entry/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">
          5
        </entry>
        <entry/>
        <entry/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">
          10
        </entry>
        <entry/>
        <entry/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">
          20
        </entry>
        <entry/>
        <entry/>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which values will complete the table for \(f(x)\)? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>8, 11, 14, 17
  </item>
  <item>
    8, 20, 35, 65
  </item>
  <item>
    3, 15, 30, 60
  </item>
  <item>
    8, 15, 30, 60
  </item>
</list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 8, 20, 35, 65</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following sets of values will complete the table for \(g(x)\)? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>
    8, 20, 35, 65
  </item>
  <item>1.1, 5.5, 10.10, 20.20
  </item>
  <item>
    1.1, 1.6, 2.59, 6.73
  </item>
  <item>
    1.1, 1.21, 1.33, 1.46
  </item>
</list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1.1, 1.6, 2.59, 6.73
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which function is linear, and which function is exponential?
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item>________ is a linear function.</item>
      <item>________ is an exponential function.</item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> a. \(f(x)\) is a linear function; b. \(g(x)\) is an exponential function.
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Functions \(m\) and \(n\) are given by \(m(x)=(1.05)^x\) and \(n(x)=\frac{5}{8}x\).
    As \(x\) increases from 0,  DROPDOWN [\(m(x)\), \(n(x)\), neither] reaches 30 first.</item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(n(x)\)
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Functions \(m\) and \(n\) are given by \(m(x)=(1.05)^x\) and \(n(x)=\frac{5}{8}x\). As
    \(x\)<emphasis> </emphasis>increases from 0, DROPDOWN [\(m(x)\), \(n(x)\), neither] reaches 100 first.</item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(m(x)\)
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>The functions \(f\) and \(g\) are defined by \(f(x)=8x+33\) and \(g(x)=2 \cdot(1.2)^x\). Which function eventually
    grows faster, \(f\) or \(g\)? Explain how you know. </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>
    \(f(x)\) grows faster because 8 is greater than 1.2.</item>
  <item>
    \(f(x)\) grows faster because 33 is greater than 2.</item>
  <item>
    \(g(x)\) grows faster because it is a linear function.
  </item>
  <item>
    \(g(x)\) grows faster because it is an exponential function.
  </item>
</list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(g(x)\) grows faster because it is an exponential function.
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>A line segment of length \(l\) is scaled by a factor of 1.5 to produce a segment with length \(m\). The new
    segment is then scaled by a factor of 1.5 to give a segment of length \(n\).
    
    What scale factor takes the segment of length \(l\) to the segment of length \(n\)? 
  </item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>2.25
</para> </section>
</content>
